\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Dear Mr. Hernandez,\
\
Thank you for your patience and for the detailed questions. Please find our replies point by point below. I have also marked the relevant parts in red on the attached drawing to make it easier to follow.\
\
1. Cage length & expansion calculation\
The cage body itself is 1.2m x 1.2m. We would suggest not dividing 17m by 5 to work out the number of sets, because the 17m house length covers more than just the cage bodies \'97 it also includes the feeding drive unit (head), the end unit (tail) and the egg collecting machine. These three items are needed only once per row: whether you run 5 sets now or expand to 90 sets later, one row shares a single head, tail and egg collecting machine. So please don't calculate capacity purely by cage length.\
\
2. Which parts must be purchased again when expanding\
On the attached quotation/drawing, every item I have marked in red is required for each additional set you add later. All the unmarked items can be shared and reused across rows. In other words, when you expand, you mainly buy the red-marked components; the rest stays as it is.\
\
3. Feeding machine travel (max 100m)\
The "travel up to 100m" refers to the maximum effective running length of one feeding machine. If your house is built to exactly 100m or less in the future, the same feeding machine can still be used. If the house exceeds 100m, a second feeding machine will be needed.\
\
4. Manure removal belt\
The manure removal system follows the same logic as the feeding system: some parts are shared (drive unit, head/tail sections), but consumable/length-dependent components such as the manure belts themselves need to be added for each new section. Please refer to the red markings on the attachment.\
\
5. House height and internal clearance\
The 4.2m we recommended is the clear internal height of the house. The 17m x 4m x 4.2m layout was confirmed by our technical team after checking the design, and walking space has already been reserved at both ends. The walkway on each side is 1m wide.\
\
6. Space per bird\
One cage houses 32 birds, and the two feed troughs together are 2.4m long, which works out to about 7.5cm of trough space per bird.\
\
Regarding the remaining civil-construction related questions, our engineer is currently away on site visits. As soon as he returns, I will confirm those details with him and send you a complete reply. Please also feel free to send over your follow-up questions on ventilation, water/manure treatment and electrical specifications \'97 we will review them together with the outstanding items above so you receive one consolidated answer instead of several partial ones.\
\
I hope this clears things up. Please let me know if any point needs further explanation.\
\
Best Regards\uc0\u65292 
